<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><posts tag="" total="3460" user="moebio"><post description="fivethirtyeight/data" extended="Data and code behind the stories and interactives at FiveThirtyEight" hash="cf1d3724416b35e2c8bb87e4984a85e4" href="https://github.com/fivethirtyeight/data" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[dataRepository] data news blog statistics" time="2015-12-16T14:59:22Z"/><post description="Favorite visualizations of Wikipedia" extended="A collection of our favorite visualizations built on Wikipedia data, curated by Hatnote." hash="55b01a003a7402aefb8aadd3e7ef7694" href="http://seealso.hatnote.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] visualization data wikipedia !" time="2015-12-10T10:53:38Z"/><post description="The 37 Best Websites To Learn Something New" extended="These websites and apps cover myriads of science, art, and technology topics. They will teach you practically anything, from making hummus to building apps in node.js, most of them for free." hash="54ee21a93c414c176e06fa1d586c2a05" href="http://observer.com/2015/11/the-37-best-websites-to-learn-something-new/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] education code [post_blog]" time="2015-11-24T15:21:10Z"/><post description="Reaching Equilibrium in Web Browser Network Visualizations" extended="Using Peano curves for placing nodes in inital good conditions before triggering a forces layout. &quot;A natural improvement over the random initial conditions would be to seed the simulation with a vertex configuration that is in the vicinity of the final destination. We could try a discretized version of the problem and search through all vertex configurations on a 2D grid.&quot;" hash="6ffc1cb9b73da9f597c2f4b35c562859" href="http://technology.quid.com/2015/11/reaching-equilibrium-in-web-browser-network-visualizations/?utm_content=23833334&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;utm_source=twitter" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] networks visualization algorithm 2d physics" time="2015-11-08T13:17:54Z"/><post description="Global Language Network" extended="Network of languages according to similarity in books or social media" hash="1a15c3275f5260b5b6fe439012270cca" href="http://language.media.mit.edu/about" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization networks language linguistics" time="2015-11-06T22:17:42Z"/><post description="Histography - Timeline of History" extended="" hash="fddc21981a68fb419e50f31b10f3f022" href="http://histography.io/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] history time visualization education !" time="2015-10-10T13:39:07Z"/><post description="Talent Lab: Superheroes" extended="data about superheroes visualized in multiple ways" hash="0c9a0916ae620e8615e80efb8956bfa8" href="http://superheroes.talent-lab.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization comics narrative statistics !" time="2015-10-02T23:18:18Z"/><post description="P vs. NP and the Computational Complexity Zoo" extended="P=NP problem incredibly well explained and contextualized" hash="e3ca20bea6cd34858fe25adca0208406" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YX40hbAHx3s" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[video_youtube] complexity math education algorithm philosophy science ! !!" time="2015-09-19T19:12:02Z"/><post description="8 Years of Dating Data" extended="a personal timeline of love affairs &quot;This chart is read left to right, up to down. Each row represents a year in my life (starting at age 15), and is made up of 365 columns representing days. You can navigate through the dates and relationships by pressing the left and right arrow keys, or by hovering over the cell you wish to investigate. A shaded purple or green cell implies that I saw the fellow in person on that day, but that data is only available for my 20s. Enjoy!&quot;" hash="e712966fb98ad5c0833d7a1cc2a1d82b" href="http://www.robinwe.is/visualizations/dating.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] time narrative psychology life ! visualization design" time="2015-09-02T13:14:30Z"/><post description="Tesla Interface Concept" extended="digital interface design for Tesla cars" hash="8a142e19c8c46d4e323af78a1098b0cb" href="https://www.behance.net/gallery/23771657/Tesla-Interface-Concept" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] interface interaction travel electricity design" time="2015-08-30T16:14:35Z"/><post description="The Obsessively Detailed Map of American Literature's Most Epic Road Trips" extended="&quot;The above map is the result of a painstaking and admittedly quixotic effort to catalog the country as it has been described in the American road-tripping literature.&quot;" hash="0883a50cdbdd5d3f4727b3fc6a9003ad" href="http://www.atlasobscura.com/articles/the-obsessively-detailed-map-of-american-literatures-most-epic-road-trips?utm_source=Atlas+Obscura&amp;utm_campaign=8c9e77d3c7-Newsletter_7_21_20157_20_2015&amp;utm_medium=email&amp;utm_term=0_62ba9246c0-8c9e77d3c7-60336825&amp;ct=t%28Newsletter_7_21_20157_20_2015%29&amp;mc_cid=8c9e77d3c7&amp;mc_eid=99efab9785" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization map travel literature text USA language gis" time="2015-08-02T14:19:27Z"/><post description="Connectome" extended="A connectome is a comprehensive map of neural connections in the brain, and may be thought of as its &quot;wiring diagram&quot;. More broadly, a connectome would include the mapping of all neural connections within an organism's nervous system." hash="6b7698c6d0745bcf04eedd03e33ba5ec" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Connectome" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] biology brain psychology networks" time="2015-07-30T13:29:29Z"/><post description="Connectogram" extended="Connectograms are graphical representations of connectomics, the field of study dedicated to mapping and interpreting all of the white matter fiber connections in the human brain. These circular graphs based on Diffusion MRI data utilize graph theory to demonstrate the white matter connections and cortical characteristics for single structures, single subjects, or populations." hash="66a12ec7ba2e76f45723eeb4f861946d" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Connectogram" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] brain visualization science biology networks graph psychology" time="2015-07-30T13:27:58Z"/><post description="Wo Europas Bevölkerung wächst – und wo sie schrumpft" extended="Detailed map of europeans regions of population growth and decline" hash="45ef65cf454d4cf52e5106b20c6d7ae5" href="http://interaktiv.morgenpost.de/europakarte/#5/48.356/11.294/en" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization map Europe population statistics ! !! city" time="2015-06-29T13:14:28Z"/><post description="Figures for &quot;Impossible fractals&quot;" extended="collection of impossible (a la Escher) fractals" hash="6cf4c8a957abfd5dd7d24ed40c5c2756" href="http://www.cameronius.com/graphics/impossible-fractals-figures/?utm_content=buffer760ef&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;utm_source=twitter.com&amp;utm_campaign=buffer" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] fractals art geometry paradox" time="2015-06-18T19:20:31Z"/><post description="Move over, Turing" extended="Machine Learning algorithms analyzing Rorschach images &quot;To understand the subconscious thoughts of the new mechanical brains, we turned to an old standby. We made them stare at inkblots and tell us what they saw.&quot;" hash="c27a56ab0e99f65907729d8e34a0a366" href="https://medium.com/@hint_fm/move-over-turing-23b26027f049" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] algorithm image humor ! brain psychology" time="2015-06-17T20:34:04Z"/><post description="How To Draw Mushrooms On An Oscilloscope With Sound - YouTube" extended="" hash="0ef69dacaa42b1a69363e1d75871d1a2" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rtR63-ecUNo" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[video_youtube] [video] frequencies geometry digital_arts sound music math visualization !" time="2015-06-08T18:49:48Z"/><post description="tree" extended="generative 3D tree" hash="ac53b7a14485012d02938c4a40ffb532" href="http://grgrdvrt.com/miam/treee/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] 3d tree fractals interaction" time="2015-05-29T11:54:52Z"/><post description="Neural-Network" extended="3D illustration of connected neurons (very inaccurate, yet cool)" hash="71b4de9bec4a66b175c05332337bf341" href="http://nxxcxx.github.io/Neural-Network/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] 3d visualization brain networks graph interface" time="2015-05-14T13:10:02Z"/><post description="typedrummer" extended="type something! (and share the beat) // percussion sequencer based on characters" hash="117ba7356da46a04755c4202c93dad33" href="http://typedrummer.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] sound toy digital_arts interface interaction music text ! !!" time="2015-05-08T21:07:30Z"/><post description="creatures avoiding planks" extended="Simple model of &quot;boids&quot; learning (by survival and reproduction) to avoid killer planks" hash="b9ea03973a0c6eb45590bc72bf540dfa" href="http://otoro.net/planks/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] biology artificial_life digital_arts interaction math complexity games design ! !!" time="2015-05-07T13:37:23Z"/><post description="Great Github list of public data sets - Data Science Central" extended="datasets meta repository, great compillation" hash="d1ca6158cc7b005ce8a85851be88c764" href="http://www.datasciencecentral.com/profiles/blogs/great-github-list-of-public-data-sets" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[dataRepository] [compilation] science data opendata networks biology economy algorithm" time="2015-04-23T17:45:24Z"/><post description="WebCanvas - The World's Largest Collaborative Painting" extended="huge collective painted canvas" hash="ee2d8c1c465631feae77d1232fdb5be6" href="http://webcanvas.com/#-42576,12853,2" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] 2d art image digital_arts ! !! space interface" time="2015-03-27T02:53:13Z"/><post description="Circles Sines and Signals - Introduction" extended="&quot;This text is designed to accompany your study of introductory digital signal processing.1 It’s an eccentric piece of not-so-rigorous literature with a preoccupation for explaining things using interactive visualizations, animations and sound.&quot;" hash="a4af570f10d9fd0ff61a9c8ff0f0fd78" href="http://jackschaedler.github.io/circles-sines-signals/index.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] education sound algorithm interaction math geometry music frequencies" time="2015-03-20T16:09:53Z"/><post description="Jorge Luis Borges and mathematics" extended="The literary works of Argentine author Jorge Luis Borges contain references to several ideas in modern mathematics. These include notions such as set theory, recursion, chaos, and infinite sequences,[1] although Borges' strongest links to mathematics are through Georg Cantor's theory of infinite sets. The title of Borges' short story &quot;The Aleph&quot; is an allusion to Cantor's use of the Hebrew letter aleph (\aleph) to denote cardinality of transfinite sets.[2] In particular, some of Borges' most popular works such as &quot;The Library of Babel&quot;, &quot;The Garden of Forking Paths&quot;, &quot;The Aleph&quot;, &quot;The Approach to Al-Mu'tasim&quot; illustrate his use of mathematics." hash="f57ebbf1461221f4ee4e03fa56615b0f" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorge_Luis_Borges_and_mathematics" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] math philosophy narrative paradox geometry ! !!" time="2015-03-03T01:32:16Z"/><post description="Sound localization" extended="Sound localization refers to a listener's ability to identify the location or origin of a detected sound in direction and distance. It may also refer to the methods in acoustical engineering to simulate the placement of an auditory cue in a virtual 3D space (see binaural recording, wave field synthesis)." hash="bb076decfd6eb4b75b9c14cf2d4e6aa7" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound_localization#Sound_localization_by_the_human_auditory_system" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] sound music psychology neuroscience physics geometry" time="2015-02-24T19:15:07Z"/><post description="Universities" extended="Timeline of Universities, starting 970" hash="99e564e397b650cdaff452fb5b97e635" href="http://histropedia.com/timeline?z7kb59m2lz" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization time history education" time="2015-02-19T16:53:11Z"/><post description="soft question - Visually stunning math concepts which are easy to explain - Mathematics Stack Exchange" extended="Some mathematical proofs and ideas clarified by clever images and animations" hash="6aced2e6bf9bdbd85322c756b79dad9d" href="http://math.stackexchange.com/questions/733754/visually-stunning-math-concepts-which-are-easy-to-explain" private="no" shared="yes" tag="math visualization [compilation] education" time="2015-02-19T14:13:12Z"/><post description="¡Dios mío! Porqué nadie ha dicho nada . Estos 100 trucos me cambiaron la vida. | Newsner" extended="100 trucos prácticos" hash="929dc2302cde811639a39e64c879d703" href="http://www.newsner.com/es/2014/08/dios-mio-porque-nadie-ha-dicho-nada-estos-100-trucos-me-cambiaron-la-vida/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] humor education [post_blog]" time="2015-02-15T16:48:27Z"/><post description="WAY TO GO" extended="beautiful game/interactive experience" hash="584105569a36e8050dc84f735f9ba076" href="http://a-way-to-go.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] 3d digital_arts games interaction education media ! !!" time="2015-02-05T23:21:55Z"/><post description="HTML5 Genetic Algorithm Biped Walkers" extended="This observational pastime hopes to evolve walking creatures through genetic algorithms." hash="9991819ac445a54962ee74ea1174a627" href="http://rednuht.org/genetic_walkers/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] genetics simulation interaction biology math digital_arts ! !!" time="2015-02-04T22:08:29Z"/><post description="The Seven Basic Plots" extended="The Seven Basic Plots: Why We Tell Stories is a 2004 book by Christopher Booker, a Jungian-influenced analysis of stories and their psychological meaning." hash="6e716ab3ef24261dd10aa60720c59b5f" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Basic_Plots" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[book] [wikipedia_article] language narrative psychology sociology" time="2015-01-25T16:31:51Z"/><post description="A dreadful start (@wnd_go) | Twitter" extended="&quot;Please don't let this happen to me&quot; an adventure on Twitter (Choose your Own Adventure style)" hash="79a367db7b9366eb4ec0d41b00e7780e" href="https://twitter.com/wnd_go" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[game] [project] twitter bifurcation digital_arts narrative hypernarrative humor" time="2015-01-13T17:56:22Z"/><post description="Big History" extended="Big History is an emerging academic discipline which examines history from the Big Bang to the present. It examines long time frames using a multidisciplinary approach based on combining numerous disciplines from science and the humanities,[1][2][3][4][5] and explores human existence in the context of this bigger picture.[6] It integrates studies of the cosmos, Earth, life and humanity using empirical evidence to explore cause-and-effect relations,[7][8] and is taught at universities[9] and secondary schools[10] often using web-based interactive presentations.[10] It is an academic movement spearheaded by historian David Christian of Australia's Macquarie University, who coined the term Big History,[7][9][11] and is made of an &quot;unusual coalition of scholars&quot;.[2] Some academic historians are skeptical about its value or originality." hash="7d77b34766a3313e3d8e271eaa4cb551" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_History" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] history time biology evolution physics ! science" time="2014-12-11T00:29:52Z"/><post description="Big History Project" extended="Can your students see the &quot;big picture?&quot; With the Big History Project, a free, open, Common Core-aligned course, you'll help students synthesize complex information, and better understand people, civilizations, and the world we live in." hash="0c5dd9431bed0b1f15309e78c867ed3a" href="https://www.bighistoryproject.com/home#" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization education time history science biology physics chemistry evolution ! !!" time="2014-12-11T00:22:22Z"/><post description="How To Learn A Language In 3 Months - Business Insider" extended="&quot;TONS of amazing resources I never even knew existed, including the best free apps and websites for becoming fluent in record time. Want to find a native speaker to help you for $5 per hour? Free resources and memory tricks? It's all here.&quot;" hash="aec78b4d86a109752be5b6708d4ff3f0" href="http://www.businessinsider.com/how-to-learn-a-language-in-3-months-2014-11" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] language linguistics tool travel ! education" time="2014-12-01T14:25:27Z"/><post description="Periodic Table of Elements" extended="amount of different atoms in different objects (human, sun…)" hash="0a7ef3844a1afdbc4607656eec38fc32" href="http://research.google.com/bigpicture/elements/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] chemistry physics visualization education ! !! biology astronomy" time="2014-11-26T21:00:03Z"/><post description="23 maps and charts on language - Vox" extended="compilation of maps and charts about languages in the world" hash="4a93c27cdd27fe78328ff7eed37fcd9a" href="http://www.vox.com/2014/11/17/7082317/language-maps-charts" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] [compilation] linguistics language map !" time="2014-11-18T11:19:48Z"/><post description="Memory Underground - Convert Your Memories Into A Subway Map - Home" extended="I have always liked the aesthetic of a subway map. There’s something about the web of colorful lines connected by dots trying so desperately to bring order to chaotic systems such as the NYC Subway or the London Underground. As an experiment, I thought it would be fun to try to automatically generate a transportation map given some set of arbitrary data." hash="ac1a8fa700720b318b9963cc9be78927" href="http://memoryunderground.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization memory travel ! !! digital_arts" time="2014-11-14T18:40:20Z"/><post description="Domino: Extracting, Comparing, and Manipulating Subsets across Multiple Tabular Datasets" extended="a novel multiform visualization technique for effectively representing subsets and the relationships between them. By providing comprehensive tools to arrange, combine, and extract subsets, Domino allows users to create both common visualization techniques and advanced visualizations tailored to specific use cases." hash="7f999b865b3e4d76d761e64d85b8d0b6" href="http://caleydo.org/publications/2014_infovis_domino/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization tool information ! !!" time="2014-11-11T19:39:10Z"/><post description="Inspirograph" extended="interactive spirograph" hash="d5f5f51e4b120ba7ddcdb5df251e6975" href="http://nathanfriend.com/inspirograph/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] toy interface interaction geometry education ! art digital_arts" time="2014-11-02T20:16:08Z"/><post description="WebGL Fluid Experiment" extended="beautiful field and particles co-interaction" hash="7b0ba42502f4c444d4f3ed496f6edf04" href="http://haxiomic.github.io/GPU-Fluid-Experiments/html5/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] physics geometry interface interaction math algorithm digital_arts" time="2014-10-27T20:23:31Z"/><post description="Donella Meadows" extended="was a pioneering American environmental scientist, teacher and writer. She is best known as lead author of the influential book The Limits to Growth, which made headlines around the world." hash="23728257e6c40709bd97877790a609d1" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donella_Meadows" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] [person] networks ecology economy complexity education" time="2014-10-14T03:50:57Z"/><post description="Endosymbiotic theory" extended="The endosymbiotic theory states that several key organelles of eukaryotes originated as symbioses between separate single-celled organisms. According to this theory, mitochondria and plastids (e.g. chloroplasts), and possibly other organelles, represent formerly free-living bacteria that were taken inside another cell as an endosymbiont, around 1.5 billion years ago. Molecular and biochemical evidence suggest that the mitochondrion developed from proteobacteria (in particular, Rickettsiales, the SAR11 clade,[1][2] or close relatives) and the chloroplast from cyanobacteria." hash="ea95beb98742c9dce5e2738b86ac7b48" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Endosymbiotic_theory" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] biology ecology evolution life emergence science complexity" time="2014-10-14T03:49:33Z"/><post description="Lynn Margulis" extended="was an American biologist and University Professor in the Department of Geosciences at the University of Massachusetts Amherst.[1][3] She developed a theory of the origin of eukaryotic organelles, and contributed to the endosymbiotic theory, which is now generally accepted for how certain organelles were formed. She showed that animals, plants, and fungi all originated from Protists. She is also associated with the Gaia hypothesis, based on an idea developed by the English environmental scientist James Lovelock." hash="7eba34647926421682c5d98d8236cad4" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynn_Margulis" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] [person] science biology education ecology emergence evolution life" time="2014-10-14T03:48:40Z"/><post description="Ada Lovelace" extended="was an English mathematician and writer chiefly known for her work on Charles Babbage's early mechanical general-purpose computer, the Analytical Engine. Her notes on the engine include what is recognised as the first algorithm intended to be carried out by a machine. Because of this, she is often described as the world's first computer programmer." hash="54d6e9730e1e8944401afc0207d0cb3b" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ada_Lovelace" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] history code technology algorithm math [person]" time="2014-10-14T03:47:04Z"/><post description="An Interactive Guide To The Fourier Transform" extended="Fourier transformation very well explained, using interactive visualization" hash="6161c74b9cc05146e759c917a56e25ff" href="http://betterexplained.com/articles/an-interactive-guide-to-the-fourier-transform/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] math education frequencies sound science ! interaction visualization" time="2014-10-13T18:21:28Z"/><post description="Visualizing MNIST: An Exploration of Dimensionality Reduction - colah's blog" extended="different techniques to dimensions reduction analyzed and visualzied" hash="402d79c17d37858b855f22c65496cc3a" href="http://colah.github.io/posts/2014-10-Visualizing-MNIST/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] visualization education geometry algorithm math image statistics ! !!" time="2014-10-10T15:32:03Z"/><post description="BIOLOGY 4 KIDS" extended="&quot;It's not just biology for kids, it's for everyone. If you are looking for basic biology help and information, stay on this site. We have information on cell structure, cell function, scientific studies, plants, vertebrates, invertebrates, and other life science topics.&quot;" hash="d1ed1791f3c466e2cf4f15d32ac564c5" href="http://www.biology4kids.com/index.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="biology science education genetics" time="2014-10-09T17:25:39Z"/><post description="Twelve leverage points" extended="The twelve leverage points to intervene in a system were proposed by Donella Meadows, a scientist and system analyst focused on environmental limits to economic growth. The leverage points, first published in 1997, were inspired by her attendance at a North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA) meeting in the early 1990s where she realized that a very large new system was being proposed but the mechanisms to manage it were ineffective." hash="42d95aae539e9762ade6e3235a88b20d" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve_leverage_points" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] ecology networks politics sociology social_network emergence science" time="2014-10-07T22:43:03Z"/><post description="Live coding in VR" extended="I built a live-coding web app for the Oculus Rift where you code in JavaScript using Three.js and watch the world change around you in real-time. This only works in the Firefox build since it uses CSS 3D for the code editor." hash="9f0a72ee89cbfdad1fb724503cecb4f0" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=db-7J5OaSag" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[video_youtube] code interface interaction algorithm 3d javascript" time="2014-10-05T13:10:47Z"/><post description="Centrality" extended="In graph theory and network analysis, centrality refers to indicators which identify the most important vertices within a graph. Applications include identifying the most influential person(s) in a social network, key infrastructure nodes in the Internet or urban networks, and super spreaders of disease. Centrality concepts were first developed in social network analysis, and many of the terms used to measure centrality reflect their sociological origin." hash="d7a3a60f0262fc0bc99a8246a40ebdb7" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centrality" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] networks graph numbers statistics" time="2014-10-03T17:08:47Z"/><post description="The Traveling Salesman with Simulated Annealing" extended="algorithm that partially solves the traveling salesman problem" hash="e3ca0287079d1c68f8fff08fcf3b4094" href="http://toddwschneider.com/posts/traveling-salesman-with-simulated-annealing-r-and-shiny/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] math graph interaction algorithm education" time="2014-10-01T11:12:46Z"/><post description="Markov Chains" extended="Visual interactive explanation of Markov Chains" hash="2eb48e47b82b394129a54a351f0ec264" href="http://setosa.io/blog/2014/07/26/markov-chains/index.html?utm_content=bufferdbf48&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;utm_source=twitter.com&amp;utm_campaign=buffer" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] math education networks graph interaction ! algorithm" time="2014-10-01T11:11:24Z"/><post description="Mobile Infovis - Mobile Information Visualisation Patterns" extended="Gallery of screen captures from visualizations on mobiles" hash="bff4e9d69273620b0010472911279765" href="http://mobileinfovis.com/smartphone" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] interface design visualization" time="2014-09-17T13:07:08Z"/><post description="GOOOOOAAAAAAAALLLLLL! | The Economist" extended="Our interactive chart depicts every goal in previous World Cup games by minute, whether from open play, a penalty or an own goal (including extra time)." hash="65a9867588beb738fe64ba6e700e9f36" href="http://www.economist.com/node/21603828" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] interaction visualization time sport history" time="2014-09-15T21:30:12Z"/><post description="Our World in Data" extended="Visualising the Empirical Evidence on how the World is Changing: health, food, violence, poverty (mostly good news). Series of statistics and visualizations" hash="d064e2e738128439a386c9209f4401a7" href="http://www.ourworldindata.org/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] economy sociology map statistics visualization history ! !! data" time="2014-09-12T12:34:22Z"/><post description="Ohio is a piano" extended="The premise is simply that each of the 88 piano keys is assigned to a single Ohio county. How the keys are mapped to the counties depends on a specified data attribute: the notes and counties are ordered by that attribute and then linked to one another. For example, if the chosen attribute is population, the county with the lowest population is assigned the lowest-frequency piano key, the county with the highest population is assigned the highest-frequency piano key, and so on." hash="b72f2d0b1d64ed311c69991d755c53d3" href="http://www.cartogrammar.com/flash/piano/Piano.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] map statistics economy visualization USA humor digital_arts interaction music sound" time="2014-09-04T15:33:49Z"/><post description="Transboundary surface water flow visualization" extended="Almost two-thirds of the countries in the world have rivers flowing into their territories from upstream countries. In an effort to visualize the surface water flowing from one country to the other (note: this does not include flows to the ocean), AQUASTAT has prepared two different chart types: - Sankey charts - Network charts" hash="9ddcaeeb3801075f3c568ba645d1a993" href="http://www.fao.org/nr/water/aquastat/flow_vis/index.stm" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization economy ecology water networks flow !" time="2014-09-03T18:55:36Z"/><post description="25 Gifs That Teach You Math Concepts Better Than Your Teacher Did" extended="collection of animated gifs featuring math concepts" hash="c74c861877c82887014d1e99e08b6286" href="http://news.distractify.com/geek/math-can-be-fun/?v=1" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] [post_blog] math geometry education animation" time="2014-08-13T12:36:44Z"/><post description="Graph drawing" extended="Graph drawing is an area of mathematics and computer science combining methods from geometric graph theory and information visualization to derive two-dimensional depictions of graphs arising from applications such as social network analysis, cartography, and bioinformatics. A drawing of a graph or network diagram is a pictorial representation of the vertices and edges of a graph. This drawing should not be confused with the graph itself: very different layouts can correspond to the same graph.[2] In the abstract, all that matters is which pairs vertices are connected by edges. In the concrete, however, the arrangement of these vertices and edges within a drawing affects its understandability, usability, fabrication cost, and aesthetics. The problem gets worse, if the graph changes over time by adding and deleting edges (dynamic graph drawing) and the goal is to preserve the user's mental map." hash="90a8b9edd7cbbaad2edf04cd4c95a98b" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_visualization" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] networks graph visualization math information" time="2014-08-11T14:29:17Z"/><post description="Rocky Morphology" extended="comparative visualization of 6 Rocky movies" hash="482641905093a088e7081990dfa4c527" href="http://fathom.info/rocky/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization movies popular video photography narrative time ! !!" time="2014-08-06T20:12:36Z"/><post description="Markov Chains" extended="visual/interactive explanation of Markov chains" hash="a369b03b0a10c52db932da9948a673e8" href="http://setosa.io/blog/2014/07/26/markov-chains/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] math visualization networks graph education !" time="2014-07-29T16:09:27Z"/><post description="Urban Observatory Compare Cities" extended="Map of 3 different cities compared, with social/economic indicators visualized &quot;an interactive exhibit that gives you the chance to compare and contrast data from cities around the world–all from one location. &quot;" hash="1b9cf5c5708b4928928837ccce249c22" href="http://urbanobservatory.org/compare/index.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] map city visualization demographics economy gis" time="2014-07-28T12:15:51Z"/><post description="bookpickings.brainpickings.org/?utm_content=buffer697cf&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;utm_source=twitter.com&amp;utm_campaign=buffer" extended="books selection by Maria Popova" hash="291f89d2e7e2bf3f493e4da198417cb9" href="http://bookpickings.brainpickings.org/?utm_content=buffer697cf&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;utm_source=twitter.com&amp;utm_campaign=buffer" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] books design narrative text science" time="2014-07-25T19:48:02Z"/><post description="Floodwatch" extended="project that tracks and visualizes the ads you see as you browse the internet" hash="045ba75f26d884fe22e63c1991426d47" href="http://floodwatch.o-c-r.org/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization time advertising internet interface !" time="2014-07-24T20:02:41Z"/><post description="Text Visualization Browser" extended="A Visual Survey of Text Visualization Techniques" hash="590f985eb1a14ed5549fb086ab9fa010" href="http://textvis.lnu.se/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] visualization text linguistics" time="2014-07-23T20:27:54Z"/><post description="A Day in the Life" extended="Visualization of Taxi activity in NY" hash="7ead7c8e7b33ae136211b9fcf426dab4" href="http://nyctaxi.herokuapp.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization New-York_USA travel map animation ! time" time="2014-07-15T11:05:23Z"/><post description="Visualizing Algorithms" extended="Post by Micke Bostock about visualizing algorithms &quot;Algorithms are a fascinating use case for visualization. To visualize an algorithm, we don’t merely fit data to a chart; there is no primary dataset. Instead there are logical rules that describe behavior. This may be why algorithm visualizations are so unusual, as designers experiment with novel forms to better communicate.&quot;" hash="2252f77fc0fd75c410c66de58f716ff0" href="http://bost.ocks.org/mike/algorithms/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] visualization code algorithm math geometry ! !! education" time="2014-06-26T15:21:44Z"/><post description="NFL players: height &amp; weight over time" extended="animated density scatter plot of height x weight in NFL throughout time" hash="03af2699d1278bbbce881f68b12cb3e1" href="http://noahveltman.com/nflplayers/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization sport statistics time !" time="2014-06-04T14:50:45Z"/><post description="Cross Industry Standard Process for Data Mining" extended="CRISP-DM (Cross Industry Standard Process for Data Mining) is an iterative data mining process model that describes commonly used approaches that data mining experts use to tackle problems." hash="be6c5537399c39413b40850ed2519be0" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross_Industry_Standard_Process_for_Data_Mining" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] information data science visualization algorithm business" time="2014-06-02T16:42:41Z"/><post description="100 Interesting Data Sets for Statistics" extended="Looking for interesting data sets? Here's a list of more than 100 of the best stuff, from dolphin relationships to political campaign donations to death row prisoners." hash="0c247a306f197ed7d34798963dd48d08" href="http://rs.io/2014/05/29/list-of-data-sets.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[dataRepository] [post_blog] [compilation] data statistics information !" time="2014-05-29T21:09:03Z"/><post description="Looking At Tears Under A Microscope Reveals A Shocking Fact." extended="One day Rose-Lynn Fisher wondered if her tears of grief would look different from her tears of joy, so she began to explore them up close under a microscope. She studied 100 different tears and found that basal tears (the ones that our body produces to lubricate our eyes) are drastically different from the tears that happen when we are chopping onions." hash="f43b62f205f89e48146cacc2a744ee0a" href="http://www.lifebuzz.com/tears/#!LCtqM" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] photography fractals patterns science psychology chemistry" time="2014-05-10T01:36:58Z"/><post description="The Bohemian Bookshelf" extended="The Bohemian Bookshelf is an information visualization that was designed to facilitate open- ended exploration of digital library collections and serendipitous book discoveries." hash="a9b74efa56da8bdcdd19e0d72a1c68f6" href="http://www.alicethudt.de/BohemianBookshelf/index.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] books visualization narrative interface interaction !" time="2014-05-02T12:57:45Z"/><post description="How Different Groups Spend Their Day - Interactive Graphic - NYTimes.com" extended="The American Time Use Survey asks thousands of American residents to recall every minute of a day. Here is how people over age 15 spent their time in 2008." hash="8317880fdd0e3d06860c9fad29a05a24" href="http://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2009/07/31/business/20080801-metrics-graphic.html?_r=0" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] sociology time flow visualization USA statistics" time="2014-04-25T22:04:41Z"/><post description="WordWanderer – take your text for a walk" extended="We are experimenting with visual ways in which we can enhance people's engagement with language. By fusing the information we can obtain from corpus searches, concordance outputs and word clouds we are aiming to enable and encourage people to notice and wander through the words they read, write and speak. To try an early prototype of the WordWanderer just select a text below or paste your own and take it for a walk." hash="840ebb6c0c1f1b9c53768194d8dcbdac" href="http://wordwanderer.org/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] interface interaction text statistics visualization tool ! !!" time="2014-04-25T19:03:05Z"/><post description="nested hypertextual Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus" extended="nested interactive navigable tree of the Tractatus by Wittgenstein" hash="0fa5fb707509b01a7c6df7e0cc624e39" href="http://www.tractatuslogico-philosophicus.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] books philosophy tree interaction bifurcation !" time="2014-04-25T03:02:41Z"/><post description="How to choose a projection" extended="guide to choose map projection &quot;Even with all you've learned about map projections, you may feel that you still don't know how to pick a good one—that is, a projection that meets your needs. So what makes a projection good? Two main things.…&quot;" hash="8ea4f40cb4f1f7f61f36926f1efbd725" href="http://www.geo.hunter.cuny.edu/~jochen/gtech201/lectures/lec6concepts/map%20coordinate%20systems/how%20to%20choose%20a%20projection.htm" private="no" shared="yes" tag="geography geometry map visualization" time="2014-04-24T22:46:59Z"/><post description="Visual Programming Languages - Snapshots" extended="visual compilation of visual programming languages" hash="022d3268e6d8378880409d7a4186024e" href="http://blog.interfacevision.com/design/design-visual-progarmming-languages-snapshots/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] [post_blog] visualization code tool interface" time="2014-04-21T12:15:16Z"/><post description="Hundreds of Exoplanets, A Handful Right for Life" extended="Of the 1,780 confirmed planets beyond our solar system, as many as 16 are located in their star’s habitable zone, where conditions are neither too hot nor too cold to support life. Size also matters: A planet that’s too small can’t maintain an atmosphere; one that’s too large will have a crushing atmosphere. A newly detected planet 493 light-years from Earth, Kepler-186f is close to Earth's size and is located in its solar system's habitable zone." hash="84ee7bbed801849aaa7c767ebe4a869b" href="http://news.nationalgeographic.com/news/2014/04/140417-exoplanet-interactive/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] astronomy interaction visualization information science education" time="2014-04-18T14:09:25Z"/><post description="The Image, Deconstructed | Examining the mental approach behind images" extended="chocking photojournalism images, with context, history and analysis" hash="8ac14f7ca39fac4757946e90f16889ce" href="http://www.imagedeconstructed.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation] photography sociology narrative !" time="2014-04-16T17:29:08Z"/><post description="Betweenness centrality" extended="a measure of a node's centrality in a network. It is equal to the number of shortest paths from all vertices to all others that pass through that node. Betweenness centrality is a more useful measure (than just connectivity) of both the load and importance of a node. The former is more global to the network, whereas the latter is only a local effect. Development of betweenness centrality is generally attributed to sociologist Linton Freeman, who has also developed a number of other centrality measures.[1] The same idea was also earlier proposed by mathematician J. Anthonisse, but his work was never published.[2] Over the past few years, betweenness centrality has become a popular strategy to deal with complex networks. Applications include computer and social networks,[3][4] biology,[5][6] transport,[7] [8] scientific cooperation[9] and so forth." hash="7140f1c3ff74262daaa573a1cdef4de8" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betweenness_centrality" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] networks math graph sociology" time="2014-04-14T21:09:57Z"/><post description="Visualizing the Bechdel test" extended="Bechdel test was enunciated by Alison Bechdel on the comic Dykes to Watch Out For in 1985. For a movie to pass the test, it must meet the following prerequisites: 1 It has to have at least two women in it, 2 who talk to each other, 3 about something besides a man." hash="3fe2be3466580ce5456bd40f6b08a06a" href="http://tenchocolatesundaes.blogspot.com.br/2013/06/visualizing-bechdel-test.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] movies information statistics visualization sociology culture data" time="2014-04-08T13:49:44Z"/><post description="Barnes–Hut simulation" extended="The Barnes–Hut simulation (Josh Barnes and Piet Hut) is an algorithm for performing an n-body simulation. It is notable for having order O(n log n) compared to a direct-sum algorithm which would be O(n2). The simulation volume is usually divided up into cubic cells via an octree (in a three-dimensional space), so that only particles from nearby cells need to be treated individually, and particles in distant cells can be treated as a single large particle centered at the cell's center of mass (or as a low-order multipole expansion). This can dramatically reduce the number of particle pair interactions that must be computed." hash="4b471f7ccc5f236c91687785f82ca499" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnes%E2%80%93Hut_simulation" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] simulation algorithm visualization physics code geometry 2d 3D" time="2014-04-02T12:50:03Z"/><post description="Curse of dimensionality" extended="The curse of dimensionality refers to various phenomena that arise when analyzing and organizing data in high-dimensional spaces (often with hundreds or thousands of dimensions) that do not occur in low-dimensional settings such as the three-dimensional physical space of everyday experience." hash="708495bcbed9b5a9248059f4c19f9a09" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curse_of_dimensionality" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] statistics math code information visualization 3D geometry topology" time="2014-04-01T16:33:52Z"/><post description="Where everyone in the world is migrating—in one gorgeous chart - Quartz" extended="In a study released in today’s Science, a team of geographers used data snapshots to create a broad analysis of global migrations over 20 years." hash="446964ad559421025adba2958d00ea1e" href="http://qz.com/192440/where-everyone-in-the-world-is-migrating-in-one-gorgeous-chart/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization circle population economy sociology geography statistics" time="2014-03-28T16:36:15Z"/><post description="List of cognitive biases" extended="" hash="7cb898766369062e1f516f9eb7bcc74f" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_biases_in_judgment_and_decision_making" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] [compilation] psychology science sociology ! !! brain" time="2014-03-27T15:11:52Z"/><post description="Patatap" extended="sound/animation online toy" hash="c2b7739a7c64cdb41f82d5c15032dd9e" href="http://www.patatap.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] animation interface interaction digital_arts ! games design sound" time="2014-03-25T23:28:04Z"/><post description="Conway's Game of Life" extended="colorful Game of Life in the browser" hash="063920a404a75cc30feee83117be4fb1" href="http://conwaygame.tk/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] interface interaction math geek complexity games life_game artificial_life color" time="2014-03-19T20:09:49Z"/><post description="DragonDrop" extended="manipulable (anchor points draggable) Dragon Fractal" hash="33ad0ff1ece5fe7a6edb9d3b9db29dd8" href="http://www.cake23.de/DragonDrop.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] fractals interaction complexity education geometry digital_arts" time="2014-03-18T17:09:24Z"/><post description="10 Dataviz Pinterest users that you must follow | Visual Loop" extended="So, we decided to pull together a quick list of Pinterest “power-users” that are very familiar to most of us that keep up with the data visualization field. - See more at: http://visualoop.com/12165/10-dataviz-pinterest-users-that-you-must-follow#sthash.6Nk3tWe2.dpuf" hash="eff69edc8a9bb3e8b938a176907b974f" href="http://visualoop.com/12165/10-dataviz-pinterest-users-that-you-must-follow" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[meta_compilation] [compilation] [post_blog] visualization image" time="2014-03-18T14:40:10Z"/><post description="Unbelievable…These 23 Mind Blowing Facts Will DESTROY Your Understanding Of Time | Distractify" extended="" hash="091266a09bc6c3f213c2e501464ffbf5" href="http://distractify.com/geek/trivial-facts/12-astonishing-facts-that-will-warp-your-perception-of-timeforever/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] time [compilation] humor history sociology technology" time="2014-03-17T00:51:12Z"/><post description="Cognitive bias" extended="A cognitive bias is a pattern of deviation in judgment, whereby inferences about other people and situations may be drawn in an illogical fashion.[1] Individuals create their own “subjective social reality” from their perception of the input.[2] An individual’s construction of social reality, not the objective input, may dictate their behaviour in the social world.[3] Thus, cognitive biases may sometimes lead to perceptual distortion, inaccurate judgment, illogical interpretation, or what is broadly called irrationality." hash="7bc2c6fbf4a58ece863b957fab10d326" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_bias" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] psychology perception philosophy brain sociology" time="2014-03-17T00:49:19Z"/><post description="List of cognitive biases" extended="Cognitive biases are tendencies to think in certain ways. Cognitive biases can lead to systematic deviations from a standard of rationality or good judgment, and are often studied in psychology and behavioral economics. Although the reality of these biases is confirmed by replicable research, there are often controversies about how to classify these biases or how to explain them.[1] Some are effects of information-processing rules (i.e. mental shortcuts), called heuristics, that the brain uses to produce decisions or judgments. Such effects are called cognitive biases.[2][3] Biases in judgment or decision-making can also result from motivation, such as when beliefs are distorted by wishful thinking. Some biases have a variety of cognitive (&quot;cold&quot;) or motivational (&quot;hot&quot;) explanations. Both effects can be present at the same time." hash="76da321b8b37c8cb0e471a1660a38da3" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_cognitive_biases" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] [compilation] psychology brain sociology perception philosophy ! !!" time="2014-03-17T00:48:37Z"/><post description="Pencil" extended="LOGO on the browser" hash="85c023fe6e9d0ee2cd839c9011b35b16" href="http://pencilcode.net/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] education tool code ! !!" time="2014-03-08T14:53:02Z"/><post description="Music Timeline" extended="" hash="beb2e3c69b9a9c1de3bdd76d13e91209" href="http://research.google.com/bigpicture/music/#" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization popular music time ! flow" time="2014-02-22T13:17:47Z"/><post description="Periodic Table of Storytelling" extended="combinable tropes and situations arranged in a Periodic Table" hash="9116284a5d1be960c3eb696990dd32d0" href="http://designthroughstorytelling.net/periodic/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization narrative text humor" time="2014-02-20T23:00:36Z"/><post description="An F1 Run Captured in Insane 360-Degree Interactive Video" extended="The Mercedes Formula One team filmed Nico Rosberg booting his 2014 Formula One car around the famed Silverstone racetrack in Great Britain — but with six high-def camera rigs recording 360-degree video during the run." hash="64152a686d8b1bef3176a2c66c39d4f2" href="http://www.wired.com/autopia/2014/02/mercedes-f1-360/?cid=co18859324" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] video 3D sport interaction [post_blog]" time="2014-02-20T01:04:32Z"/><post description="Brain Semantic Space" extended="continual semantic space in a 3D representation of the brain (from fiducial to flat) &quot;This viewer shows how information about thousands of object and action categories is represented across human neocortex. The data come from brain activity measurements made using fMRI while a participant watched hours of movie trailers. Computational modeling procedures were used to determine how 1705 distinct object and action categories are represented in the brain.&quot;" hash="c20acbbb95f17797389f2722568bf1ac" href="http://gallantlab.org/semanticmovies/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] 3D visualization science language brain biology psychology linguistics ! !! !!! interaction interface networks tree" time="2014-02-14T19:18:48Z"/><post description="Kaizen" extended="Kaizen (改善?), Japanese for &quot;improvement&quot; or &quot;change for the best&quot;, refers to philosophy or practices that focus upon continuous improvement of processes in manufacturing, engineering, and business management. It has been applied in healthcare, psychotherapy, life-coaching, government, banking, and other industries." hash="88b1d0f65c73221b6b12c5fbcca67b58" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaizen" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] philosophy life design business Japan psychology" time="2014-02-13T21:27:53Z"/><post description="Pointer Pointer by @StudioMoniker" extended="fun photo search (ppl pointing with fingers) by cursor position" hash="826e7f2790dbf47e20c70fba5e8d682d" href="http://www.pointerpointer.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] 2d photography humor interaction ! art digital_arts" time="2014-02-04T23:39:58Z"/><post description="earth wind map" extended="real time wind map of the world" hash="d71d043ca03a4e956a4bce1cbaff048e" href="http://earth.nullschool.net/#current/wind/surface/level/stereographic=69.22,162.67,135" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] climate geography geometry visualization animation flow map ! !! 3D" time="2014-01-15T12:36:15Z"/><post description="100 Years of Rock Visualized" extended="timeline + genders influence network, playable samples" hash="56746e220130fcba465675f3e49a591b" href="http://www.concerthotels.com/100-years-of-rock" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization time music popular ! !! history networks art collage" time="2014-01-09T18:27:11Z"/><post description="40 years of ATP ranking" extended="" hash="bd101c6b190b2f5f6abf79337d4f3dab" href="http://1.bp.blogspot.com/-XN_mwnJPOOQ/Ur731teuBWI/AAAAAAAABxg/TiEbVqJ11e0/s1600/heatmap_atpRanking300.png" private="no" shared="yes" tag="visualization [image] sport history statistics time" time="2014-01-06T13:01:06Z"/><post description="Lexical Distance Among the Languages of Europe" extended="This chart shows the lexical distance — that is, the degree of overall vocabulary divergence — among the major languages of Europe. The size of each circle represents the number of speakers for that language. Circles of the same color belong to the same language group. All the groups except for Finno-Ugric (in yellow) are in turn members of the Indo-European language family" hash="59eebebd71155a97037199f64955bc78" href="http://elms.wordpress.com/2008/03/04/lexical-distance-among-languages-of-europe/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="visualization networks language Europe linguistics" time="2014-01-05T13:40:01Z"/><post description="Erik Zachte's Wikipedia / Wikimedia Portfolio" extended="Visualizations and data from Wikipedia | Wikipedian since 2002. Data Analyst at Wikimedia Foundation since 2008." hash="3b40a158b1de789661525935c48203fd" href="http://infodisiac.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[portfolio] data information visualization statistics wikipedia ! !!" time="2013-12-28T11:28:31Z"/><post description="Odysseus' Journey" extended="Interactive Map of Homer's Odysseus' Journey" hash="0af12e4ff811dee825451e08b11f5d5c" href="http://esripm.maps.arcgis.com/apps/MapTour/index.html?appid=4fc9153f4d9248b9bab7011e3950b552" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project] visualization map literature history narrative geography ! Greece" time="2013-12-17T16:13:09Z"/><post description="List of common misconceptions" extended="common misconceptions in science, history…" hash="200061b03d9320b892dc114122c2b212" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_common_misconceptions" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article] history science sociology [compilation]" time="2013-12-13T15:32:26Z"/><post description="Distancia_a_la_costa.png (1278×619)" extended="world map of iso distances to shore" hash="ab73627fa70e7bc4a5528140a1db6ca1" href="http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/Distancia_a_la_costa.png" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[image] map sea geometry !" time="2013-12-05T20:05:27Z"/><post description="25 mapas que nos ayudan a entender de increíbles formas nuestro planeta (IMÁGENES) « Pijamasurf" extended="25 interesting global maps featuring different indexes and statistics" hash="1495de81593ba14c51a83d0f9b9f145b" href="http://pijamasurf.com/2013/11/25-mapas-que-nos-ayudan-a-entender-de-increibles-formas-nuestro-planeta-imagenes/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] [compilation] map statistics economy sociology humor" time="2013-12-02T12:59:41Z"/><post description="GBD Compare" extended="Causes of death, global and local, by Institute for Health and Metrics and Evaluation" hash="d63e48c84df190594e4baf782265a3f6" href="http://viz.healthmetricsandevaluation.org/gbd-compare/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  health  tree  statistics  !  " time="2013-11-26T18:44:31Z"/><post description="Global Forest Change" extended="high-resolution world map of forests loss and regeneration" hash="81f98998c64d8c9c8a9c0ea9800deacc" href="http://earthenginepartners.appspot.com/science-2013-global-forest" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  map  ecology  biology  tree  !  " time="2013-11-14T20:44:04Z"/><post description="What Are Traffic Waves and Why Do They Happen So Much? | The Lowdown" extended="&quot;To illustrate this concept, programmer Lewis Lehe, a civil engineering graduate student at UC Berkeley, created this visualization.

Select a car from the bunch, click “Hit the Brakes” to slow down your highlighted car, and then wait until a traffic wave forms. The red bars represent deceleration levels (braking) and the green,  acceleration (speeding up). Mouse over a car to slow everything down see its velocity and acceleration at any given point during the wave (assuming all the cars are in the same single lane).&quot;" hash="e28a757d608d8a5a06d8d1ed42221908" href="http://blogs.kqed.org/lowdown/2013/11/12/traffic-waves" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  travel  emergence  animation  interaction  science  !  " time="2013-11-14T17:33:50Z"/><post description="Cartes étymologiques de mots en Europe" extended="etymological maps of different words in Europe" hash="61997356dfb1140d7d08e61ee3701638" href="http://www.laboiteverte.fr/cartes-etymologiques-de-mots-en-europe/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog]  linguistics  map  visualization  !  !!  geography  " time="2013-11-11T12:59:07Z"/><post description="StoryMap JS - Telling stories with maps" extended="online tool to add contents to routes in maps and tell stories" hash="7788cb9154c475876a135af7b0bfc3f5" href="http://storymap.knightlab.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  map  narrative  visualization  tool  interface  !  !!  " time="2013-11-06T15:32:53Z"/><post description="ChronoZoom" extended="interactive timeline with information about cosmos, earth, life and humanity" hash="72168dd4bef031263968903f3a496495" href="http://www.chronozoom.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  time  history  biology  geology  astronomy  physics  education  interface  visualization  !  !!  " time="2013-11-06T14:19:39Z"/><post description="11 Untranslatable Words From Other Cultures | Ella Frances Sanders" extended="11 &quot;wonderful, untranslatable, if slightly elusive, words&quot; in different languages" hash="f5793f98af2497d79c7d33293a8c73f1" href="http://www.huffingtonpost.com/ella-frances-sanders/11-untranslatable-words-f_b_3817711.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] illustration [compilation] language" time="2013-11-05T13:34:04Z"/><post description="How Many Earths?" extended="Interactive animation and visualization that explains how many exoplanets can exist" hash="767a402547deb68ef59ce0753620c6bb" href="http://exoplanets.newscientistapps.com/?utm_source=Twitter&amp;utm_medium=napsm&amp;utm_campaign=Hootsuite" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  astronomy  education  narrative  !  " time="2013-10-30T14:44:21Z"/><post description="NoFlo | Flow-Based Programming for JavaScript" extended="NoFlo is a JavaScript implementation of Flow-Based Programming (FBP). Separating the control flow of software from the actual software logic. Helping you organize large applications easier than traditional OOP paradigms, especially when importing and modifying large data sets." hash="4d577e5fdde0f3f375976bccede8b36a" href="http://noflojs.org/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  code  tool  graph  interface  !  !!  " time="2013-10-18T17:34:06Z"/><post description="8 Subconscious Mistakes Our Brains Make Every Day--And How To Avoid Them" extended="I was seriously shocked at some of these mistakes in thinking that I subconsciously make all the time. Obviously, none of them are huge, life-threatening mistakes, but they are really surprising and avoiding them could help us make more rational, sensible decisions." hash="6af0344aed025a88d5e57677bf60c13e" href="http://www.fastcompany.com/3019903/work-smart/8-subconscious-mistakes-our-brains-make-every-day-and-how-to-avoid-them" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] sociology psychology economy brain" time="2013-10-16T16:18:23Z"/><post description="a closer look at Fractals - Spirals" extended="There are some fundamental shapes in nature and one of them is the spiral - from the DNA helix to galactic nebulae they can be found in sheer endless variety and across all size scales.

One might expect that the abstract world of mathematics is equally full of spirals and within that the classic fractals of the Mandelbrot and Julia sets also contain many different forms and across all scales as well.

But there are some surprises in how this all happens - and while you may well have seen fractal imagery for years, it is the small details that are worth examining.

This little essay is trying to bring some of these unusual visual forms to light, show examples of the variety and oddity as well - and and muse about their implications a bit…" hash="c2c408fbaf0f57671855bbcb1ec296f6" href="http://blog.fract.al/post/62180302979/a-closer-look-at-fractals" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog]  spiral  geometry  fractals  image  shape  " time="2013-10-09T10:10:29Z"/><post description="Noisli - background noise and color generator for working and relaxing" extended="Noisli is a fantastic and good looking background noise and color generator ideal for working and relaxing. It includes many high-quality sounds to help you focus while working, relief anxiety or to just relax while reading or before going to sleep." hash="25a18d70687f16edd301685c2095a48d" href="http://www.noisli.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  sound  music  collage  design  !  toy  " time="2013-10-08T23:34:08Z"/><post description="Code Webs - Visualizing 40,000 student code submissions" extended="The above figure is the landscape of ~40,000 student submissions to the same programming assignment on Coursera's Machine Learning course. Nodes represent submissions and edges are drawn between syntactically similar submissions. Colors correspond to performance on a battery of unit tests (with red submissions passing all unit tests)." hash="bfdd531d411692ddb657a01905b0a69a" href="http://www.stanford.edu/~jhuang11/research/pubs/moocshop13/codeweb.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  networks  code  algorithm  !  graph  " time="2013-10-08T16:40:19Z"/><post description="World War II in Europe: Every Day" extended="Territory conquests (Europe, Africa), day by day" hash="f3f1226a8e0d77663b9c31a1ab2123e8" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WOVEy1tC7nk" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[video_youtube]  violence  history  time  space  2d  visualization  education  !  Europe  Africa  map  " time="2013-10-03T19:32:13Z"/><post description="Patterns in nature" extended="Patterns in nature are visible regularities of form found in the natural world. These patterns recur in different contexts and can sometimes be modelled mathematically. Natural patterns include symmetries, trees, spirals, meanders, waves, foams, arrays, cracks and stripes.[1] Early Greek philosophers studied pattern, with Plato, Pythagoras and Empedocles attempting to explain order in nature. The modern understanding of visible patterns developed gradually over time." hash="47d6a4d99f7b0d021243f5b857bbcd4e" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patterns_in_nature" private="no" shared="yes" tag="patterns [wikipedia_article] ! physics chemistry !! shape biology geometry" time="2013-10-02T00:14:22Z"/><post description="The America’s Cup Finale: Oracle’s Path to Victory" extended="USA versus New Zealand, America's cup paths visualized" hash="2ea52770330199a56389fc3cf968ae3b" href="http://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/09/25/sports/americas-cup-course.html?_r=0" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  time  sport  sea  !  !!  " time="2013-09-26T12:23:55Z"/><post description="wait but why: Putting Time In Perspective" extended="
a series of timelines in different time zooms

To try to grasp some perspective, I mapped out the history of time as a series of growing timelines—each timeline contains all the previous timelines (colors will help you see which timelines are which).  All timeline widths are exactly accurate to the amount of time they're expressing." hash="738b35e81a4766624aadb031059138c6" href="http://www.waitbutwhy.com/2013/08/putting-time-in-perspective.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog]  visualization  time  history  !  !!  zoom  " time="2013-09-14T15:55:19Z"/><post description="Constellations of Directors and Their Stars" extended="&amp;quot;A long-running relationship between an actor and a director can indicate an artistic understanding, a functional routine or even a marketing strategy. Here, a selection of notable directors are shown with actors they cast in at least four films.&amp;quot; by NYTimes.com" hash="e3201293a4152db746148b89192b38bd" href="http://www.nytimes.com/newsgraphics/2013/09/07/director-star-chart/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="networks visualization ! graph popular movies [project] interaction !!" time="2013-09-07T15:08:31Z"/><post description="40 Maps That Will Help You Make Sense of the World" extended="collection of interesting (some weird) maps of the world" hash="f622b79bd9c05d3b8fdad3dd47d72dca" href="http://twistedsifter.com/2013/08/maps-that-will-help-you-make-sense-of-the-world/?utm_content=buffer724a1&amp;utm_source=buffer&amp;utm_medium=facebook&amp;utm_campaign=Buffer" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] visualization geography sociology [compilation] economy map" time="2013-09-05T12:57:07Z"/><post description="Text to ASCII Art Generator (TAAG)" extended="ASCII text generator with lots of typographies" hash="b08dfdbecc8dd7d2681f06b8501f774d" href="http://patorjk.com/software/taag/#p=display&amp;f=Abraxis-Big&amp;t=I%20prefer%20pi" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  text  tool  technology  black_and_white  geek  typography  " time="2013-08-10T16:23:44Z"/><post description="GearSketch" extended="simple gear creation tool" hash="240e6414f2087e2e7381e4a3197f79a0" href="http://www.gearsket.ch/#b77048ad8dce6c55" private="no" shared="yes" tag="! toy physics tool [project] circle education simulation" time="2013-08-04T13:55:08Z"/><post description="Hatnote Listen to Wikipedia" extended="sonorization of wikipedia editions in real time" hash="357070a43d71e855f61909e90d55e333" href="http://listen.hatnote.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  music  sound  wikipedia  circle  !  !!  visualization  time  " time="2013-08-02T19:08:14Z"/><post description="Culturomics" extended="Culturomics is a form of computational lexicology that studies human behavior and cultural trends through the quantitative analysis of digitized texts.[1][2] Researchers data mine large digital archives to investigate cultural phenomena reflected in language and word usage." hash="1f3c9d749c134f2c09e50e9918b56a52" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culturomics" private="no" shared="yes" tag="books [wikipedia_article] sociology literature culture algorithm statistics" time="2013-07-26T14:13:07Z"/><post description="Cultural analytics" extended="Cultural Analytics is the exploration and research of massive cultural data sets of visual material - both digitized visual artifacts and contemporary visual and interactive media. Taking on the challenge of how to best explore large collections of rich cultural content, Cultural Analytics researchers developed new methods and intuitive visual techniques which rely on high-resolution visualization and digital image processing. These methods are used to address both the existing research questions in humanities, to explore new questions, and to develop new theoretical concepts which fit the mega-scale of digital culture in the early 21st century." hash="ab6320ebcba19921ca8faea91de52340" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_analytics" private="no" shared="yes" tag="books visualization [wikipedia_article] popular literature culture algorithm statistics" time="2013-07-26T14:11:52Z"/><post description="Anscombe's quartet" extended="Anscombe's quartet comprises four datasets that have nearly identical simple statistical properties, yet appear very different when graphed." hash="1ecb006e117aa06fd4635536e73961ab" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anscombe's_quartet" private="no" shared="yes" tag="math visualization [wikipedia_article] numbers 2d geometry statistics" time="2013-07-25T12:29:38Z"/><post description="Webgraph" extended="The webgraph describes the directed links between pages of the World Wide Web. A graph, in general, consists of several vertices, some pairs connected by edges. In a directed graph, edges are directed lines or arcs. The webgraph is a directed graph, whose vertices correspond to the pages of the WWW, and a directed edge connects page X to page Y if there exists a hyperlink on page X, referring to page Y." hash="eb14438734a38927059fd44a3cb3758b" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Webgraph" private="no" shared="yes" tag="networks technology graph [wikipedia_article] complexity internet" time="2013-07-14T20:23:46Z"/><post description="Social network" extended="A social network is a social structure made up of a set of social actors (such as individuals or organizations) and a complex set of the dyadic ties between these actors. The social network perspective provides a clear way of analyzing the structure of whole social entities. The study of these structures uses social network analysis to identify local and global patterns, locate influential entities, and examine network dynamics." hash="bb8cd53dfbe2a36158d9abd6d4b86cdb" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network" private="no" shared="yes" tag="networks [wikipedia_article] graph complexity sociology social_network economy culture" time="2013-07-14T20:22:04Z"/><post description="Semantic network" extended="A semantic network, or frame network, is a network which represents semantic relations between concepts. This is often used as a form of knowledge representation. It is a directed or undirected graph consisting of vertices, which represent concepts, and edges." hash="64a11922fbc4867fa40750caf3480c27" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semantic_network" private="no" shared="yes" tag="text graph technology [wikipedia_article] complexity social_network internet linguistics networks" time="2013-07-14T20:11:48Z"/><post description="Protein–protein interaction" extended="Protein–protein interactions occur when two or more proteins bind together, often to carry out their biological function. Many of the most important molecular processes in the cell such as DNA replication are carried out by large molecular machines that are built from a large number of protein components organised by their protein–protein interactions. Protein interactions have been studied from the perspectives of biochemistry, quantum chemistry, molecular dynamics, chemical biology, signal transduction and other metabolic or genetic/epigenetic networks. Indeed, protein–protein interactions are at the core of the entire interactomics system of any living cell." hash="c3605f54757b1ded006f04a743f31483" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protein-protein_interaction" private="no" shared="yes" tag="networks [wikipedia_article] graph life chemistry complexity bacteria interaction 3D biology geometry" time="2013-07-14T20:10:01Z"/><post description="Scale-free network" extended="A scale-free network is a network whose degree distribution follows a power law, at least asymptotically. The most important and common kind of networks find in nature and culture." hash="d1cfc24d4fb5c97501505fef9af17a9b" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scale-free_network" private="no" shared="yes" tag="math networks information ! graph [wikipedia_article] social_network randomness !! topology geometry" time="2013-07-14T18:49:39Z"/><post description="Preferential attachment" extended="A preferential attachment process is any of a class of processes in which some quantity, typically some form of wealth or credit, is distributed among a number of individuals or objects according to how much they already have, so that those who are already wealthy receive more than those who are not. &quot;Preferential attachment&quot; is only the most recent of many names that have been given to such processes. They are also referred to under the names &quot;Yule process&quot;, &quot;cumulative advantage&quot;, &quot;the rich get richer&quot;, and, less correctly, the &quot;Matthew effect&quot;. It is related to Gibrat's law. The principal reason for scientific interest in preferential attachment is that it can, under suitable circumstances, generate power law distributions." hash="f93f922380762dffef45d48e02e780ab" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preferential_attachment" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[wikipedia_article]  math  statistics  algorithm  networks  randomness  " time="2013-07-13T13:55:25Z"/><post description="Data Science Toolkit" extended="compilation of tools for geocoding, sentiment analysis and data analysis in general" hash="e3a2323bd33c2c2f814f62501cbe9dd1" href="http://www.datasciencetoolkit.org/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation]  tool  data  information  technology  code  statistics  algorithm  " time="2013-07-12T01:19:54Z"/><post description="Kit FUI - Noteloop" extended="Fantasy User Interfaces, Fictional User Interfaces, Fake User Interfaces, Futuristic User Interfaces. Regardless of what the F stands for, they all represent the same thing, the user interfaces and heads up displays found in many popular movies and television shows." hash="ab67d63f794a3e4fab229807dcac9793" href="http://www.noteloop.com/kit/fui/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation]  interface  interaction  future  design  movies  tv  science_fiction  technology  popular  " time="2013-07-11T15:44:27Z"/><post description="Hypertext" extended="Hypertext is text displayed on a computer display or other electronic device with references (hyperlinks) to other text that the reader can immediately access, usually by a mouse click, keypress sequence or by touching the screen. Apart from text, hypertext is sometimes used to describe tables, images and other presentational content forms with hyperlinks. Hypertext is the underlying concept defining the structure of the World Wide Web. It enables an easy-to-use and flexible connection and sharing of information over the Internet." hash="125f4e666defd2a9d666df738d90d5e4" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext" private="no" shared="yes" tag="networks technology [wikipedia_article] bifurcation internet hypernarrative" time="2013-07-10T13:14:29Z"/><post description="Pale Fire" extended="Pale Fire (1962) is a novel by Vladimir Nabokov. The novel is presented as a 999-line poem titled &amp;quot;Pale Fire&amp;quot;, written by the fictional John Shade, with a foreword and lengthy commentary by a neighbor and academic colleague of the poet, Charles Kinbote. Together these elements form a narrative in which both authors are central characters. Pale Fire has spawned a wide variety of interpretations and a large body of written criticism, which Pekka Tammi estimated in 1995 as over 80 studies." hash="3a2490e19c14d42878ebfa9e1e369b11" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pale_Fire" private="no" shared="yes" tag="books text [wikipedia_article] narrative [book] bifurcation hypernarrative" time="2013-07-10T13:08:36Z"/><post description="How Simple Can Life Get? It’s Complicated" extended="&quot;Life does not exist in a laboratory vacuum, where scientists can pare away genes to some Platonic purity. Life exists in a tapestry, and the species with the smallest genomes in the world survive only because they are nestled in life’s net.&quot;" hash="84fb27f1dc1376b4d60fb5eec935ed7d" href="http://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/04/science/how-simple-can-life-get-its-complicated.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog]  biology  genetics  ecology  complexity  information  !  !!  " time="2013-07-04T13:41:29Z"/><post description="Welcome to History Mesh" extended="Explore a woven world of technological whimsy. Start with our suggested stories, or choose a random node to dive in." hash="f26eacf7a8bd6d02093cb36b579dd70d" href="http://historymesh.com/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  history  time  technology  network  education  !  !!" time="2013-07-03T20:58:03Z"/><post description="The Art market for Dummies" extended="Does Mark Rothko mean anything to you ? Neither does Natalia Goncharova ? Don’t panic! Who are the most bankable artists ? Which paintings everyone wants to buy ? What is the Art capital of the world ? Find the answers in these infographics and become an Art market expert !" hash="217a831702a84883d09ce278fbfb1992" href="http://quoi.info/wp-content/uploads/data_art/en/#.UdHTNhYxW83" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[post_blog] visualization ! sociology art economy [project] business" time="2013-07-01T19:11:34Z"/><post description="Dictionary of the Khazars" extended="Dictionary of the Khazars: A Lexicon Novel (Serbian: Хазарски речник / Hazarski rečnik) is the first novel by Serbian writer Milorad Pavić, published in 1984. Originally written in Serbian, the novel has been translated into many languages. It was first published in English by Knopf, New York in 1988.[1]
There is no easily discerned plot in the conventional sense, but the central question of the book (the mass religious conversion of the Khazar people) is based on an historical event generally dated to the last decades of the 8th century or the early 9th century when the Khazar royalty and nobility converted to Judaism, and part of the general population followed." hash="e2a83700ad94927220a67fa008fc0ab1" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_the_Khazars" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[book]  [wikipedia_article]  books  narrative  bifurcation  text  time  Serbia" time="2013-07-01T15:42:24Z"/><post description="HHWC: Champagne Always Stains My Silk" extended="occurrences of word &quot;champagne&quot; in rap music throught time" hash="0b696525caab1616d47ec227487a8fff" href="http://staplecrops.com/champagne/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  music  text  USA  popular  humor  narrative  time  !  " time="2013-06-26T15:59:31Z"/><post description="Wikipedia Worldview" extended="map of languages of Wikipedia" hash="1cb57ee59636f00d3ca9a850553fa8f5" href="http://www.wikipediaworldview.net/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  wikipedia  map  linguistics  interaction  geography  " time="2013-06-20T16:41:55Z"/><post description="Interactive Feature: Molecular Origami" extended="Here's an interactive feature that illustrates the basic idea of DNA origami by letting you manipulate a biomolecule, such as DNA or RNA. The general idea is the same as DNA origami, but instead of adding staple strands to the scaffold, you define the binding sequences on scaffold, keeping in mind how you'd like the strand to fold. " hash="847a12f73ca911fadb6c695b3caba9d7" href="http://wyss.harvard.edu/viewpage/m-o/interactive-feature-molecular-origami" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  origami  science  interface  chemistry  genetics  life  biology  interaction  education  !  " time="2013-06-15T12:40:55Z"/><post description="Terry Gilliam's Do It Yourself Animation Show" extended="Terry Gilliam (Monty Python animation based on collage) teaching animation techniques with cutouts" hash="cf4076d1190a8ee871e7b47518cab84e" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IrsKPKjGF_Y&amp;feature=player_embedded" private="no" shared="yes" tag="! black_and_white animation tv art popular tutorial collage humor UK education [video_youtube]" time="2013-06-01T13:12:55Z"/><post description="Hapax legomenon" extended="a word that occurs only once within a context, either in the written record of an entire language, in the works of an author, or in a single text. The term is sometimes incorrectly used to describe a word that occurs in just one of an author's works, even though it occurs more than once in that work. Hapax legomenon is a transliteration of Greek ἅπαξ λεγόμενον, meaning &amp;quot;(something) said (only) once&amp;quot;." hash="e986c31365eafb7930dbe90abc11389c" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hapax_legomenon" private="no" shared="yes" tag="text [wikipedia_article] literature linguistics statistics" time="2013-05-28T19:55:30Z"/><post description="MONIAC Computer" extended="The MONIAC (Monetary National Income Analogue Computer) also known as the Phillips Hydraulic Computer and the Financephalograph, was created in 1949 by the New Zealand economist Bill Phillips (William Phillips) to model the national economic processes of the United Kingdom, while Phillips was a student at the London School of Economics (LSE). The MONIAC was an analogue computer which used fluidic logic to model the workings of an economy. The MONIAC name may have been suggested by an association of money and ENIAC, an early electronic digital computer." hash="7f922e9613c3d1f63c656d3af6c503d0" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MONIAC_Computer" private="no" shared="yes" tag="history technology [wikipedia_article] economy UK flow code" time="2013-05-24T21:21:31Z"/><post description="cubes.io: 3d cellular automata" extended="3D cellular automata" hash="4a0900f1fc6294a23f08876739a4932a" href="http://cubes.io/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="math ! cellular_automata tool [project] interaction 3D interface" time="2013-05-16T11:26:22Z"/><post description="Words that last" extended="A research team led by Mark Pagel at the University of Reading in England has identified 23 “ultraconserved words” that have remained largely unchanged for 15,000 years." hash="9761ce35d6aa554b87a61ffbf7750c70" href="http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/special/national/words-that-last/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  sound  linguistics  history  information  sociology  " time="2013-05-09T00:36:03Z"/><post description="Data Visionary - DIY" extended="
DIY compilation of creative activities for children related with data.

Data is just a series of measurements, but locked inside is a story. Data Visionaries transform rows of numbers into understandable visual stories. They seek to illustrate compelling facts that can change our thinking and reveal new decisions for our future." hash="bf84ea155003005263a6eab3fc63c737" href="https://diy.org/skills/datavisionary" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[compilation]  data  information  visualization  education  games  video  tutorial  !  !!  " time="2013-05-03T14:11:21Z"/><post description="Every Noise at Once" extended="This is an algorithmically-generated non-analytical map of the musical genre-space. Genres and artists are positioned by code and data, adjusted for legibility, but the underlying vectors are less interesting than the juxtapositions and clusters that they produce, so the axes have been deliberately left unlabeled and uncalibrated. You are invited to imagine your own qualities and magnitudes that the geometry might be expressing." hash="3bf623abfd3580d3183bb24e9f5c5534" href="http://www.furia.com/misc/genremaps/engenremap.html" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  music  sound  network  interface  history  !  !!  !!!" time="2013-05-03T13:04:58Z"/><post description="PathFinding.js" extended="path finder algorithms visualized" hash="388e9fca89029606b761a602359224e7" href="http://qiao.github.io/PathFinding.js/visual/" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  visualization  algorithm  geometry  space  2d  interaction  code  " time="2013-05-02T18:36:00Z"/><post description="TweetMap ALPHA" extended="95 million tweets from 12/10/2012 to 12/31/2012

