odel of Eades and Kamada-Kawai; the term force-directed comes from Fruchterman &amp; Reingold's 1990 University of Illinois technical report (UIUCDCS-R-90-1609)." hash="c7c201dd2c9657519706a9fe9d5db9d8" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Force-based_algorithms_(graph_drawing)" private="no" shared="yes" tag="math ! algorithm physics graph networks visualization [wikipedia_article]" time="2012-10-18T19:37:16Z"/><post description="Gallery of named graphs" extended="Some of the finite structures considered in graph theory have names, sometimes inspired by the graph's topology, and sometimes after their discoverer. A famous example is the Petersen graph, a concrete graph on 10 vertices that appears as a minimal example or counterexample in many different contexts." hash="f32c1b0af9b98f18352c7994016f2e5d" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gallery_of_named_graphs" private="no" shared="yes" tag="!!! !! ! [compilation] networks topology geometry math graph [wikipedia_article]" time="2012-10-18T17:54:05Z"/><post description="Horton graph" extended="In the mathematical field of graph theory, the Horton graph or Horton 96-graph is a 3-regular graph with 96 vertices and 144 edges discovered by Joseph Horton.[1] Published by Bondy and Murty in 1976, it provides a counterexample to the Tutte conjecture that every cubic 3-connected bipartite graph is Hamiltonian." hash="5cbcc57d29b02c3f3c05658c4133a108" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horton_graph" private="no" shared="yes" tag="topology geometry geo math networks graph [wikipedia_article]" time="2012-10-18T17:53:00Z"/><post description="GIFCTRL: Next Level Gif Control" extended="animated gifs controlled by mouse position" hash="58d904e76367361434dcde6e3c8724d5" href="http://gifctrl.com/fw" private="no" shared="yes" tag="[project]  humor  games  image  animation  photography  " time="2012-10-17T21:48:42Z"/><post description="Welcome to DataThief" extended="DataThief extracts data from graphs (reverse engineering)" hash="8cbfa37